#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e6a31e-57dc-533f-972b-bdf2882f3e24" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Perimeter"}] :question/id #uuid "ed569da8-3409-5fe1-8ef1-066e28479e53" :question/math-academy-id "e-347" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a string of length $20$ inches and that we shape it to make a circular sector, as shown in the diagram below.  Among all of the circular sectors that can be made with that string, what's the radius of the one that gives the largest area?\n\n![](images/04/0473dd96f4f5f27c03d0ecbbba7ed0c0458527e71f515e242ec790d1dfea2d09.png)" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ stand for the circular sector's radius and let $\\theta$ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter of our sector is fixed at $20$ inches in length, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n20=2r + r\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that $s=r\\theta$ gives the arc-length $s$ of a circular sector of radius $r$ and central angle $\\theta,$ where $\\theta$ is in radians.\n\nNow, by making $\\theta$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{20-2r}{r} = \\dfrac{20}{r} -2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nA(r) &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\dfrac{20}{r} -2\\right)\\[3pt]\n&= 10r -r^2.}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nA'(r) = 10 -2r.\n$$\n\nSetting $A'(r) = 0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 10-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=5$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nA''(r) = -2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad A''(5) = -2 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince $A''(5)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=5$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the greatest area will have a radius of $5\\,\\textrm{in},$ and the maximum area is given by\n\n$$\nA(5) = 10(5) - 5^2 = 25\\,\\textrm{in}^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf444699-9f3f-5e1e-9ead-b9f2dabbdbb3" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Perimeter of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Area"}] :question/id #uuid "be781400-b8ab-5673-952c-104306590d99" :question/math-academy-id "e-348" :question/problem "Consider all circular sectors $MON$ with area $A=8$ square units. What is the angle $\\theta$ of the circular sector that has the smallest possible perimeter?\n\n![](images/c8/c8b9506069c1b1d974399ad5afb751a79c9cced3e984cd5c38725d31a045aecd.png)" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta$ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP= 2r + r\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $8$ square units,  so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8 = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n8 &= \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta\\[3pt]\n\\theta &= \\dfrac{16}{r^2}.\n}\n$$\n\nPlugging this value into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{16}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nP'(r) = 2 - \\dfrac{16}{r^2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative $P''(r) = \\dfrac{32}{r^3} > 0$, the stationary point $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum. \n\nFinally, when $r=2\\sqrt{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the angle $\\theta = 2$ radians minimizes the perimeter of the circular sector $MON.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf444699-9f3f-5e1e-9ead-b9f2dabbdbb3" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Perimeter of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179f7f9c-7c2f-5ad0-991f-1bbdd00ec824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +2}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508424} {:answer/id #uuid "6e485939-81fc-5180-9df4-9c4801683555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\pi +4}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508555} {:answer/id #uuid "84592676-b46c-5846-b30f-ce8c1c8efa34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{\\pi +2}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508586} {:answer/id #uuid "be8d6e20-b2d5-5f4a-9454-c6990f20a7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508678} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c619ff-7a41-5aee-a283-e50b36fb5436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be8d6e20-b2d5-5f4a-9454-c6990f20a7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a81c85c-e7bb-5c20-a42f-21dc54ac708f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92add898-4b49-5d01-bf37-847e609cbb26" :question/math-academy-id "q-15089" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9b79e710dda94e6c4d91a157317977f5b2388202bb63b08fcc325ea93f0453c.png)\n\nA gardener wants to create a flowerbed in the shape of a rectangle topped by a semicircle, as shown in the diagram. He needs the flowerbed to have an area of $\\frac{25}{9}\\,{cm}^{2}.$ What is the radius $r$ of the flowerbed with the smallest possible perimeter?" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $x$ the side of the rectangle.\nThen, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=\\pi r+2x+2r.\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $\\frac{25}{9}$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}=2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{9} & =2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 2rx & =\\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\pi r+2x+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+2(\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4})+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+\\frac{25}{9r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{2}+2r \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)r+\\frac{25}{9r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 9{r}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{50}{9(\\pi +4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{50}{9{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e6a31e-57dc-533f-972b-bdf2882f3e24" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Perimeter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136d2a61-b0f7-5dd5-8838-447c8e8d76e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508416} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1c04e1-50cd-5cdc-bde9-38e71d892e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508456} {:answer/id #uuid "5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508524} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ef677-0dfb-56b7-b72e-a9eb57ef63cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{100}{\\pi +4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508530} {:answer/id #uuid "e79c2f39-3294-5d2a-83ce-47805402dd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{300}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508524} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d6be88-7869-5ea2-874d-dbe10543c41d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d85466ba-cd37-5739-bfc9-c486bbfacc9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-17957" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0da2ed0bca11725e7ffb3fa3c5952807e406530a0625b01ebb476f0145b64e30.png)\n\nA farmer wants to fence a field that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. He has only $200\\,m$ of fencing. What is the length $2x$ of the side of the field that covers the greatest area?" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ the radius of the upper semicircle (top) and $x$ the radius of the lateral semicircle (right), hence the dimensions of the rectangle are $2r$ and $2x.$ We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(2r)(2x)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $200$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n200=\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}200 & =\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r \\\\ x(\\pi +2) & =200-r(\\pi +2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4r(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =4(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+4r(-1)+\\pi r+\\pi (\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{800}{\\pi +2}-4r-4r+\\pi r-\\frac{200\\pi }{\\pi +2}+\\pi r \\\\  & =-8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} & =0 \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =-\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =\\frac{200\\pi -800}{\\pi +2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100\\pi -400}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100(\\pi -4)}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-8+2\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})=-8+2\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-\\frac{100}{\\pi +2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the side of the field with the largest area is $2x=\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf444699-9f3f-5e1e-9ead-b9f2dabbdbb3" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Perimeter of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c220bd5-a7c3-540a-9148-f781412b5305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c220bd5-a7c3-540a-9148-f781412b5305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508436} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de83ee2-eca3-5af9-9209-a72f1eca0796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b59b20a8-8e7c-533f-bc4e-e028196934bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-315941" :question/problem "![](images/be/be50f6313cb20e06c3d850a3e81f267ac5e2cdc5c2a308280ba6367c08d66fec.png)\n\nMatt is planning a field whose surface is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the field must be $9\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Matt wants a field with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $9$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =9-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{18}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e6a31e-57dc-533f-972b-bdf2882f3e24" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Perimeter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe0f3a0e-95e4-5ef5-9aec-3c4c7af2e50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe0f3a0e-95e4-5ef5-9aec-3c4c7af2e50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508807} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6880c81-e1e8-5f43-89ee-3d61afafa579" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9284b07a-9db4-5c4c-b135-110855ca0f14" :question/math-academy-id "q-344681" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f17ce467f5bff34f0c04326d1a23ca8621f459571be8c98a745174a3d5587337.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=100$ units. Determine the angle $\\theta $ (in radians) of the circular sector with the largest possible area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}\nradians" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $100$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{100-2r}{r}=\\frac{100}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{100}{r}-2) \\\\  & =50r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=50-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 50-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=25$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(25)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(25)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=25$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=25$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{100}{r}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{25}-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e6a31e-57dc-533f-972b-bdf2882f3e24" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Perimeter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d68cfadd-4957-55c7-b502-d28278bf4278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d68cfadd-4957-55c7-b502-d28278bf4278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508735} :answer-field/id #uuid "975fd3e3-b86b-5c26-9e9b-ffa0d6d69891" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e590d69-9e57-5a7b-a44f-da12f0280772" :question/math-academy-id "q-344791" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cd71fa90a1213292961960facf049a1333beb63abeb53f08ba18173281fb330.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $ABC$ with perimeter $P=80$ units. Determine the largest possible area of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe maximum area is\n{{field-1}}\nsquare units." :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $80$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n80=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{80-2r}{r}=\\frac{80}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{80}{r}-2) \\\\  & =40r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=40-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 40-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=20$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(20)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(20)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=20$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the largest area will have a radius of $20,$ and the area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =40r-{r}^{2} \\\\  & =40(20)-(20)^{2} \\\\  & =400.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e6a31e-57dc-533f-972b-bdf2882f3e24" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Perimeter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30+\\frac{60}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30+\\frac{60}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508659} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf6bf10-deb1-51fd-ba83-03a05512720e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06dbc133-9492-57ef-8ac9-643d61a66e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344853" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ea7b23d8dd14a0ed092152592800022400ee73e7b371146f5e972900f2c2f29.png)\n\nA community organizer wants to install a border around a walkway made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. She has only $120\\,m$ of border material. The organizer wishes to maximize the area of the **rectangle**. Find $x+2r$ where $x$ and $2r$ are the dimensions of the rectangle that has the greatest area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x+2r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the semicircles (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the length of the other side of the rectangle. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2rx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $120\\text{ m},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n120=\\pi r+\\pi r+2x=2\\pi r+2x.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & =2\\pi r+2x \\\\ 2x & =120-2\\pi r \\\\ x & =60-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2r(60-\\pi r) \\\\  & =120r-2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =120-4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 120-4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =120 \\\\ r & =\\frac{30}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-4\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })=-4\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =60-\\pi (\\frac{30}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =60-30 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=30$ and $2r=\\frac{60}{\\pi },$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+2r=30+\\frac{60}{\\pi }=\\frac{30\\pi +60}{\\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf444699-9f3f-5e1e-9ead-b9f2dabbdbb3" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Perimeter of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f9b529d-152c-55af-89ef-1953f5ea8685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9b529d-152c-55af-89ef-1953f5ea8685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508577} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7dafff5-b3a8-518e-8b51-5bc2eaf0b1e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c9b4d08-b984-5348-9fbd-0d702cd0c142" :question/math-academy-id "q-344931" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d21626823e213a4c2d198f42e8e66d353364e722c1add41b35fcbc138ed8bff4.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $JKL$ with area $A=121$ square units. Determine the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the smallest possible perimeter. Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $121$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n121=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}121 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{242}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =242 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =121 \\\\ r & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=11$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{484}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=11$ is a minimum.\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter is $r=11$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf444699-9f3f-5e1e-9ead-b9f2dabbdbb3" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Perimeter of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc531b63-ca0c-5196-ac8d-68aa088deb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc531b63-ca0c-5196-ac8d-68aa088deb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508675} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1618c4-780a-57b4-9a0f-35ba97b75da4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d662c12-9d76-5b11-86a1-8fd56f6d8729" :question/math-academy-id "q-344938" :question/problem "![](images/60/60e04faa7448d351706ee1af70a8620590ca16e0debce7dd2e58631462594132.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $DEF$ with area $A=144$ square units. Determine the smallest possible perimeter $P$ of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $144$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n144={r}^{2}\\frac{\\theta }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{288}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =288 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =144 \\\\ r & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{576}{{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=12$ is a minimum.\n\nFinally, when $r=12$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{288}{144} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dimensions of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter are $r=12$ and $\\theta =2.$ Therefore, the smallest perimeter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12+12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24+24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf444699-9f3f-5e1e-9ead-b9f2dabbdbb3" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Perimeter of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a859638-2f37-5243-a583-e0227a8f23fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a859638-2f37-5243-a583-e0227a8f23fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508434} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ecf20a-f4fe-5988-bf3c-7b7d82479ab8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3990dbc6-49ea-5002-bf6b-028dcf68d1bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-345016" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0ef7a2645946099b10772fdc3fee46c582ad43debb0cb53041c46aa99c203ab1.png)\n\nSofia is designing a park that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the park must be $16\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Sofia wants a park with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $16\\text{ km},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n16=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =16-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{32}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e6a31e-57dc-533f-972b-bdf2882f3e24" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Circular Sector With a Fixed Perimeter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "928c2847-7ef6-5e6c-9e86-4bdc08839ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508608} {:answer/id #uuid "c02779ce-52f0-516c-8624-96b7ce026a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$44.5$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508684} {:answer/id #uuid "c928eb10-8758-5697-8bc6-529533e2ea40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$58$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508706} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2951d6-7d10-5d24-95a2-411a03c4a3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508716} {:answer/id #uuid "fee47921-86a7-5e1e-846d-a82ad8a8f292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$54$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "928c2847-7ef6-5e6c-9e86-4bdc08839ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45$ units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508608} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc0977c-f93c-51ec-b77b-0003eb68d4f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "500619bc-56ac-575b-907b-2407a9ecc2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-51205" :question/problem "![](images/59/59949e4ba7818ea08d4613fee703a2c973ffa4953c820f04c1ae9279147dad56.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=180$ units. What is the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the largest possible area?" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $180$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n180=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{180-2r}{r}=\\frac{180}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{180}{r}-2) \\\\  & =90r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=90-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 90-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=45$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(45)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(45)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=45$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$"}]}
